the project is called cities and human  society in the 21st century  and the logic is pretty simple without  800 million people will be added in  cities all over the world in the next  two decades or so and within twenty  twenty-five years we'll have 60 cities  over ten million inhabitants cities are  the leaders of our globalizing world and  they are the avant-garde of all this  transformation so we need to really take  care of them and to understand how to  deal with that these are places where  you have an immense concentration of  power wealth Allen cultural movement  innovation going on and then to think  about these four areas that make sense  for us climate change and the  post-carbon City big data and all the  new data and knowledge where we can  produce the social division inclusion of  cities and the infrastructure and  housing bringing people together from  different urban experiences and getting  them to talk to one another and getting  them to think about how the evidence  that I know from one setting might be  similar to but also might be different  from the evidence that comes from  another setting potentially a way also  of thinking outside the box and changing  what the box is so to do that you need  something like safar which is you look  in this capacity to provide a framework  when you bring those people from  different backgrounds together to think  about knowledge that might be  transformative and as a result the  impact will hopefully be that each of  the disciplines will become more  powerful by interacting with the other  discipline successful cities will make  for a successful 21st century human  society as a whole and that's really our  our goal in our deep commitment and to  do it at a planetary or global scale  [Music]
 

